{
  "id": "Compound::DB00873 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Loteprednol, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is linked to Nr3c1 through multiple pathways that converge on the therapeutic glucocorticoid class. The evidence suggests that Loteprednol, as part of the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist family, binds to Nr3c1, a key component of the glucocorticoid receptor, which is involved in the regulation of gene expression in response to steroid hormones. This binding is supported by the presence of multiple related compounds—Desonide, Prednicarbate, and Desoximetasone—within the same class, all of which are known to interact with Nr3c1. The shared lowest common ancestors, such as Therapeutic Glucocorticoid and 3-Oxo-Delta(1),Delta(4)-Steroid, further reinforce the biological relevance of this interaction. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust and well-documented mechanism, suggesting that Loteprednol's interaction with Nr3c1 is a clinically relevant and mechanistically sound process. The evidence supports a direct role of Loteprednol in modulating Nr3c1 activity, which is critical for the biological effects of corticosteroids.",
